        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was the album \"Girls' Generation\" released?\n\nAnswer A: November 1, 2007\n  about: Girls' Generation (album)\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was released by S.M. Entertainment on November 1, 2007.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: January 1, 2006\n  about: Girls' Generation (album)\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was released by S.M. Entertainment on January 1, 2008.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Girls' Generation (2007 album) Girls' Generation () is the self-titled debut album by South Korean girl group Girls' Generation. It was released by S.M. Entertainment on November 1, 2007. A reissue titled \"Baby Baby\" was subsequently released on March 13, 2008. The tour Girls' Generation Asia Tour Into the New World was used to promote the album, alongside with music shows promotion before the tour, which started on December 19, 2009 in Olympic Fencing Gymnasium, Seoul, South Korea, ended in Taipei Arena, Taipei, Taiwan on October 17, 2010. \"Into the New World\" was initially released as Girls' Generation's debut single,\nDocument 2: Girls' Generation (2006 album) Girls' Generation () is the self-titled debut album by South Korean girl group Girls' Generation. It was released by S.M. Entertainment on January 1, 2006. A reissue titled \"Baby Baby\" was subsequently released on March 13, 2008. The tour Girls' Generation Asia Tour Into the New World was used to promote the album, alongside with music shows promotion before the tour, which started on December 19, 2009 in Olympic Fencing Gymnasium, Seoul, South Korea, ended in Taipei Arena, Taipei, Taiwan on October 17, 2010. \"Into the New World\" was initially released as Girls' Generation's debut single.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
